  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 258 PM EDT Mon Jul 20 2026\n\n- Storms Tonight\n\nA vigorous upper shortwave trough is phasing with troughing over \neastern Canada, while a deepening surface low north of Lake \nSuperior drives a cold front into a moist and unstable airmass \nacross Wisconsin. The convective allowing models show a line of\nstorms initiating in that area this afternoon then moving\nsoutheast across Lake Michigan and into western Lower Michigan\nthis evening. They also show a general weakening trend as\ninstability decreases this evening, although shear profiles remain\nimpressive.\n\nThe main threat appears to be downburst winds associated with \nbowing line segments along with isolated large hail in the\nstrongest, most persistent updrafts. The threat should be ending \nTuesday morning as the cold front pushes through.\n\n- Upper Troughing Continues through next week\n\nThe heat and humidity of last week looks to be gone for a while \nwith all the long range models showing a persistent pattern of \nridging across the SW CONUS and troughing across the NE CONUS. \nThis will keep the Great Lakes region in a northwest flow regime \nfor the next week at least, with occasional cold front passages \nthat could focus convection. The next one appears to be next \nweekend. Overall, a cooler and drier than normal weather regime \nfor the rest of the month is expected.",
